## Resumable conditions — signal a recoverable problem and let the HANDLER
## decide whether the computation carries on.
##
## The difference from `Exception` is that a condition handler may `resume`, so
## the signalling computation CONTINUES. That is what lets one run collect every
## problem instead of stopping at the first, which neither exceptions nor a
## plain `Result` can do: after a throw the frames in between are gone, and a
## `Result` needs an explicit branch at every call site.
##
## `fallback` comes from the CALL SITE, not the handler. That is what makes the
## operation polymorphic in the recovery value — each signal site offers a value
## of its own type, and one body may signal at several types:
##
##     let name = if ok then "alice" else signal { ctx = "bad name", fallback = "?" }
##     let age  = if ok then 30      else signal { ctx = "bad age",  fallback = 0 }
##
## It is also what keeps the handler honest. The operation returns `'a`, chosen
## by the site, so a handler may not invent a value of its own — it either
## carries on with what the site offered or gives up (V0503/T0210).
effect Condition having 'ctx where {
  ## Signal `ctx`, offering `fallback` as the value to carry on with.
  signal: { ctx: 'ctx, fallback: 'a } -> 'a <Condition>
}

## Carry on at every signal, using the offered fallback.
##
## The computation runs to completion and every signal is tolerated, so the
## result reflects ALL the recoveries rather than the first failure.
impl Condition for UseFallback having 'ctx = 'c where {
  signal = { ctx, fallback } -> resume { val = fallback }
}

## Give up at the first signal, with the context as the error.
##
## The `perform` block's result is a `Result`, so the body's own tail must
## produce one too — `perform FirstErr in do … Ok { val = … }`.
impl Condition for FirstErr having 'ctx = 'c where {
  signal = { ctx, fallback } -> abort { val = Err { error = ctx } }
}

## Give up at the first signal, discarding the context.
impl Condition for FirstNone having 'ctx = 'c where {
  signal = { ctx, fallback } -> abort { val = None }
}

## Signal a condition. Shorthand for `Condition.signal`.
pub let signal = { ctx, fallback } -> Condition.signal { ctx = ctx, fallback = fallback }
